Subject: [PATCH 32/32] property.fs: the cursor — compose a structure at an address the caller chose
Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc

TODO 16's second deliverable. Patch 31 made a single field writable where
the caller says; a structure is more than one field, and composing them
at a chosen address is the operation the arena words cannot do. `encode+`
concatenates in the arena -- honestly, since patch 27, even across a
moved HERE -- but the arena is exactly what the toolkit is escaping.

    int!+    ( n dest -- dest' )
    string!+ ( str len dest -- dest' )
    bytes!+  ( data-addr data-len dest -- dest' )

`( dest -- dest' )` AND NOT A CURRENT-DESTINATION VARIABLE. The cursor is
the value on the stack: two structures can be under construction at once,
in different memory, with no interleaving hazard and nothing to save and
restore. A variable would be the mode flag TODO 16 rejected, one layer
up.

They have NO caller in the device tree by construction -- the tree builds
properties through encode-*, into the arena, as it always did.

WHAT IT DEMONSTRATES, and it is the toolkit's minimum viable shape: three
fields written at a caller-chosen address, then read back with the STOCK
1275 decoder. The read half was always general (that is F2's other half:
`decode-int` takes an arbitrary prop-addr/prop-len and reads through it);
only the write half was arena-bound. Now both halves meet at an address
the caller picked:

    tgt  11111111 swap int!+  22222222 swap int!+  33333333 swap int!+
    w-advanced=c   w-HERE-UNCHANGED
    tgt c decode-int -> 11111111, 22222222, 33333333

WATCHED TO BITE, and this time the controls are scoped by construction --
the new words have no device-tree callers, so a fault in them cannot take
the firmware down before the probe runs. That was the failure the first
attempt at controlling patch 31's writers ran into.

  stride wrong (`/int 1+`)   w-advanced=f, and FIELD ONE IS STILL CORRECT:

      w-i1=11111111   w-i2=ff222222   w-i3=22ff3333

    which is why the decode assertions cover fields two and three. A
    cursor that advances wrongly writes the first field perfectly; the
    `ff` bleeding through is the buffer's poison showing in the gaps.

  cursor touches the arena    w-HERE-MOVED, with all three fields still
                              decoding correctly -- isolating the arena
                              property from the correctness property, the
                              same pairing patch 31 needed.

diff --git a/forth/device/property.fs b/forth/device/property.fs
--- a/forth/device/property.fs
+++ b/forth/device/property.fs
@@ -362,6 +362,35 @@
 
 : bytes!  ( data-addr data-len dest -- )    swap move ;
 
+\ TODO 16, the cursor. A structure is more than one field, and composing
+\ fields at a caller-chosen address is the operation the arena words cannot do:
+\ `encode+` concatenates in the arena (and since patch 27 it does so honestly
+\ even across a moved HERE), but the arena is exactly what the toolkit is trying
+\ to escape.
+\
+\ `( dest -- dest' )` AND NOT A CURRENT-DESTINATION VARIABLE. The cursor is the
+\ value on the stack; two structures can be under construction at once, in
+\ different memory, with no interleaving hazard and nothing to save and restore.
+\ A variable would be the mode flag TODO 16 rejected, one layer up.
+\
+\ These have NO caller in the device tree by construction -- the tree builds
+\ properties through encode-*, into the arena, as it always did. That is also
+\ what makes them safe to break in a control: a fault here cannot take the
+\ firmware down before the probe runs, which is the failure the first attempt at
+\ controlling the writers ran into.
+
+: int!+    ( n dest -- dest' )
+  dup >r int! r> /int +
+  ;
+
+: string!+ ( str len dest -- dest' )
+  >r 2dup /string r@ swap >r string! r> r> +
+  ;
+
+: bytes!+  ( data-addr data-len dest -- dest' )
+  >r 2dup /bytes r@ swap >r bytes! r> r> +
+  ;
+
 : encode-int    ( n -- prop-addr prop-len )
   /int alloc-tree tuck int! /int
   ;
